#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372 :topic/difficulty 0.7847057767066771 :topic/id #uuid "43f7df3c-d8d4-556e-9f46-8a4092e26bff" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068672 :question/id #uuid "2acac780-2b17-5489-accc-2a3a090784e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9927" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 + 14x + 49) - 8$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{x}^{2}+14x+49 &= {x}^{2}+14x+{7}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x+7)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n({x}^{2}+14x+49)-8 &= (x+7)^{2}-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068677 :question/id #uuid "b107ed10-4501-50ad-8ad7-231fd933bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9997" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 - 12x + 36) -5$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{x}^{2}-12x+36 &= {x}^{2}-12x+{6}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x-6)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n({x}^{2}-12x+36)-5 &= (x-6)^{2}-5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/math-academy-id "e-10008" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  + 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :question/id #uuid "06ed73b3-1442-5b21-a317-961d0395f7da" :question/math-academy-id "e-10009" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  +4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :topic/math-academy-id 450 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050044}] :topic/title "Completing the Square"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580 :topic/id #uuid "557f8b58-be49-555e-8162-3d1448eaff0c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179477 :question/id #uuid "dc5cefa9-aa18-52d3-bbdc-a0c1050f6f3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14168" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 2x-1$ for $x \\in (-1,1].$ What is its range?" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/32/32925e498e936298ada23ba1c560b992b7dc2dce0e39fd6479bf6ba5ce2cb545.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values on the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-3.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-3).$ However, the graph does not include this point.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs at the point $(1,1).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-3$ and $1,$ including $1$ but not including $-3.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-3 < y \\leq 1,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-3 < f(x) \\leq 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Linear Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179651 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179478 :question/id #uuid "c639f820-2493-5267-9f59-9bb06e58dea7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14171" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown below?\n\n![](images/88/880925c00705275ef8630444a08053bc6ca2c79b2e4754a09d0bd7a2af059f32.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs when $x\\to \\infty.$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\infty,$ but not including $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1 \\lt  y \\lt \\infty,$ which can also be written as  $-1 \\lt f(x) \\lt \\infty.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6df7104d-0f74-5301-9525-132c74c19ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Function by Considering Its End Behavior"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179479 :question/id #uuid "1d74961e-433c-5c0f-8bdb-d279c5170cc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14173" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a277f71617ce2dceae7ee97017655c4c27e57a9bb22020995905760e53bf7720.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-2,1]$ is $[-2,2].$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (1,5)$ is $(1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,2] \\cup (1,3] = [-2,3].$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6727b20-2af5-5bff-aae4-e35c44fde144" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Piecewise Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3728 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642}] :topic/title "The Range of a Function: Advanced Cases"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642 :topic/id #uuid "5ba25c95-b937-5921-95e0-7207aa7b83f4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124357 :question/id #uuid "1a085da3-d8eb-52eb-94ea-4969fb74a83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5876" :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y = \\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $g(x) = \\sqrt{3x+5} -2,$ we replace $x$ with $3x+5$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n{3x+5} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5}{3}, \\infty\\right).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f94991ce01ad98ec5bdffbb0673182dc40bc264803b6eea5154241985359c0b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124358 :question/id #uuid "bf9e3a92-47fa-5969-8d05-2d28114f3036" :question/math-academy-id "e-5877" :question/problem "Which of the following values are possible outputs of $f(x) = 2 \\sqrt{x- 2} + 2?$\n\nI. $1$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $6$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-2} \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x-2}+2 & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in [2,\\infty).$\n\nAmong the given values, only $f(x)=2$ and $f(x)=6$ lie in the range. So, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10d9a611e2fbf8ebf6ae3c12b57e51ea100bf13663586379c42293d4ee45d183.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124359 :question/id #uuid "cdb0a166-a880-5efb-a747-ac8f940ff50b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5878" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 2].$\n\n- The range  is $f(x) \\in [2, \\infty).$\n\n- The point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-x}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\le b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in \\left(-\\infty, 2\\right],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 2.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\nb=2.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{a\\sqrt{2-x}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=2.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =a\\sqrt{2-1}+2 \\\\ 4 & =a+2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2-x}+2,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124389 :question/id #uuid "fa7bd088-d496-5862-80a0-cd20083b2754" :question/math-academy-id "e-7944" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = \\sqrt{6x+7} - 5.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6x+7} - 5 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6x+7}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6x+7} & =5 \\\\ (\\sqrt{6x+7})^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ 6x+7 & =25 \\\\ 6x & =18 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6\\cdot 3+7}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1875 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050992}] :topic/title "Properties of Transformed Square Root Functions"}]}
